1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:06,219
This is GEXDASH, an open-source dashboard for
options traders. It estimates where

2
00:00:06,239 --> 00:00:10,651
dealers have to hedge, and charts
trend and signals beside it.

3
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:17,118
Step one: the Terminal view, one of three in
the sidebar. Net exposure across the

4
00:00:17,138 --> 00:00:21,229
top, candles in the middle, and the
strike profile beside them.

5
00:00:22,207 --> 00:00:28,055
Step two: dealer levels. The call and
put walls, the gamma flip, max

6
00:00:28,075 --> 00:00:31,560
pain and the largest gamma strikes
are drawn on the price chart.

7
00:00:31,861 --> 00:00:37,635
Hover a candle, and the readout shows its
open, high, low and close, with RSI and

8
00:00:37,655 --> 00:00:38,859
its average.

9
00:00:39,799 --> 00:00:45,398
Step three: exposure by strike.
Gamma, delta, vanna,

10
00:00:45,419 --> 00:00:49,251
charm, volume and open
interest each have a tab.

11
00:00:49,617 --> 00:00:54,516
The flip and spot are both marked on the
profile, so it shows how far price is from

12
00:00:54,536 --> 00:00:57,002
the point where dealer gamma changes sign.

13
00:00:57,371 --> 00:01:01,694
Who holds which side is an assumption, not a
measurement. It's a setting in the

14
00:01:01,714 --> 00:01:04,825
toolbar, and the screen
says what it assumes.

15
00:01:05,759 --> 00:01:11,391
Step four: the Columns view reads the
whole surface at once. Market bias,

16
00:01:11,553 --> 00:01:15,837
spot and levels, the level radar,
and exposure by strike.

17
00:01:16,789 --> 00:01:21,790
Step five: the Chart view, for any ticker you
type into the search, from one-minute

18
00:01:21,810 --> 00:01:23,129
to weekly bars.

19
00:01:23,475 --> 00:01:29,327
Smoothed averages from twenty to two hundred,
EMA nine, two Bollinger bands, and a

20
00:01:29,347 --> 00:01:31,710
volume profile over the range shown.

21
00:01:32,070 --> 00:01:38,290
Drawing tools run down the left: lines,
channels, Fibonacci and Gann, patterns,

22
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:42,285
measures and brushes, with a lock
and a hide for all of them.

23
00:01:43,238 --> 00:01:49,280
Step six: signals. Buy, add, trim and
sell follow one position at a time.

24
00:01:49,429 --> 00:01:53,313
Breakouts and breakdowns come from
each consolidation base.

25
00:01:53,663 --> 00:01:58,506
Every rule is written out below the chart, so
any signal can be checked by hand.

26
00:01:59,441 --> 00:02:04,847
Step seven: intraday. Five days of
fifteen-minute bars, with session

27
00:02:04,867 --> 00:02:09,949
dividers and Supertrend, ported line for
line from its TradingView script.

28
00:02:10,292 --> 00:02:15,180
Supertrend flipped back to buy on Wednesday
afternoon, and its band trails below

29
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:20,528
price. ETFs show regular hours unless
you add the extended sessions.

30
00:02:21,466 --> 00:02:26,608
Step eight: futures. ES and NQ are
adjusted for contract changes,

31
00:02:26,628 --> 00:02:32,213
as thinkorswim does, so a quarterly
roll doesn't print as a breakout.

32
00:02:32,555 --> 00:02:36,751
Each roll is marked along the foot of the
price chart, and one button switches the

33
00:02:36,771 --> 00:02:37,897
adjustment off.

34
00:02:38,859 --> 00:02:44,687
Step nine: time zone. Pick one beside the
clock, and every time on screen follows

35
00:02:44,707 --> 00:02:49,016
it, while sessions stay the
exchange's. Here, Tokyo.

36
00:02:49,999 --> 00:02:55,297
GEXDASH is open source under the MIT
licence. Run it on synthetic data, connect

37
00:02:55,317 --> 00:03:01,547
Schwab when you're ready, and remember: it's
an analysis tool, not trading advice.

